{"question_id": "test_00041", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "C", "explanation": "The patient's symptoms (tingling, numbness, pain) with decreased pain sensation and intact reflexes, along with poorly controlled diabetes (HbA1c 8.1%), indicate diabetic peripheral neuropathy. The best measure to prevent progression of neuropathy is strict blood glucose control, as chronic hyperglycemia is the primary driver of neuropathic damage. Evidence from Harrison's and other sources emphasizes glycemic management in diabetes. Atorvastatin (A) addresses dyslipidemia, not neuropathy; bypass (B) is for peripheral artery disease, which is absent here (ABPI normal); amputation (D) is not indicated.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "C", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 6868.244409561157, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1639, "output": 335}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_27756", "Physiology_Levy_3875", "Obstentrics_Williams_7946", "Gynecology_Novak_906", "InternalMed_Harrison_28324"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 1563.4968280792236, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 5304.533243179321, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1639, "output": 335}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00042", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "A", "explanation": "The presentation of bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, failure to pass meconium, and polyhydramnios in a newborn with a quadruple screen pattern suggestive of Down syndrome (decreased AFP, increased beta-hCG, decreased estriol, increased inhibin A) is most consistent with duodenal atresia. Evidence from First Aid Step 1 indicates that duodenal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension within the first 1–2 days of life and is associated with Down syndrome. The other options are less likely: meconium ileus is associated with cystic fibrosis, not Down syndrome; necrotizing enterocolitis is more common in preterm infants; and pyloric stenosis presents later with nonbilious vomiting.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "A", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 8514.584302902222, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1359, "output": 605}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_1364", "Obstentrics_Williams_5878", "Biochemistry_Lippincott_1929", "First_Aid_Step1_385", "Obstentrics_Williams_3170"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 543.280839920044, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 7971.025466918945, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1359, "output": 605}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
