{"question_id": "test_00054", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "A", "explanation": "The patient's symptoms of lip smacking, mouth pursing, and excessive blinking that developed slowly over 2 weeks while on chronic haloperidol are characteristic of tardive dyskinesia, as described in evidence [1] which links such movements to long-term antipsychotic use and recommends discontinuing haloperidol.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "A", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 8551.463603973389, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1038, "output": 239}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["First_Aid_Step2_1262", "InternalMed_Harrison_31752", "InternalMed_Harrison_2062", "InternalMed_Harrison_31753", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3232"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 1587.0907306671143, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 6964.166164398193, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1038, "output": 239}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00055", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "A", "explanation": "The presentation of an erythematous, well-demarcated patch with peripheral scale in the groin during summer is classic for tinea cruris (a fungal infection). KOH examination of lesion scrapings is the standard method to confirm dermatophyte infection by visualizing hyphae. The provided evidence does not directly address this, but standard medical practice supports this choice.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "A", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 13196.92325592041, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1225, "output": 99}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_3136", "Pediatrics_Nelson_4014", "InternalMed_Harrison_1621", "InternalMed_Harrison_15944", "Pediatrics_Nelson_2143"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 1094.3472385406494, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 12102.330923080444, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1225, "output": 99}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00056", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "C", "explanation": "Risperidone is a potent dopamine antagonist that can cause hyperprolactinemia, leading to gynecomastia. The provided evidence does not directly mention gynecomastia, but it is a well-known adverse effect of risperidone. Agranulocytosis is more associated with clozapine, not risperidone. QT interval is prolonged, not shortened, and weight gain is common, not weight loss.", "confidence": 0.8, "gold_answer": "C", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 10218.028545379639, "total_token_usage": {"input": 985, "output": 902}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Neurology_Adams_12321", "Neurology_Adams_9677", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3100", "First_Aid_Step2_1262", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3145"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 597.7504253387451, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 9620.071411132812, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 985, "output": 902}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00057", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "B", "explanation": "Toxic shock syndrome (TSS) is caused by staphylococcal superantigens (e.g., TSST-1) that bind directly to the variable β-chain of the T cell receptor, leading to massive T cell activation. Evidence [1] and [2] describe TSS as associated with tampon use and exotoxins, and superantigens are known to interact with the Vβ region of the TCR.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "B", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 7699.851274490356, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1289, "output": 413}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_4122", "Gynecology_Novak_2021", "First_Aid_Step1_115", "InternalMed_Harrison_9751", "First_Aid_Step1_325"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 666.0952568054199, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 7033.531188964844, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1289, "output": 413}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00058", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "C", "explanation": "The clinical presentation of a child with a preceding diarrheal illness, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes, elevated LDH, indirect hyperbilirubinemia), thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury (elevated BUN and creatinine) is classic for hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS), often associated with Shiga toxin-producing E. coli. This is supported by evidence [2] which describes HUS features including positive stool culture for E. coli O157:H7, shiga-toxin, and renal impairment.", "confidence": 1.0, "gold_answer": "C", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 6895.733118057251, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1340, "output": 456}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["First_Aid_Step2_887", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3450", "First_Aid_Step2_917", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1941", "Pediatrics_Nelson_2390"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 596.6224670410156, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 6298.904895782471, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1340, "output": 456}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
