{"question_id": "test_00095", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "D", "explanation": "The patient's bilateral flank masses, hypertension, family history of kidney transplant, and elevated creatinine suggest autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD). A common extrarenal complication of ADPKD is intracranial aneurysms, which can be screened for with MR angiography of the brain. This is the most appropriate test among the options to detect additional complications.", "confidence": 0.9, "gold_answer": "D", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 105440.70100784302, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1309, "output": 1160}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_3664", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1006", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1490", "InternalMed_Harrison_21957", "Pharmacology_Katzung_1127"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 626.7614364624023, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 104813.72261047363, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1309, "output": 1160}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00096", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "A", "explanation": "The patient's presentation (shortness of breath, pleuritic chest pain, hemoptysis, filling defect in pulmonary vessels) is consistent with pulmonary embolism. Evidence [3] describes a similar case with deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary emboli, and notes a family history of blood clots. Evidence [5] mentions that some familial forms of pulmonary hypertension are related to inactivating mutations of BMPR2, which is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. This matches the likely underlying cause of the patient's symptoms.", "confidence": 0.85, "gold_answer": "A", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 4200.996875762939, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1286, "output": 128}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Anatomy_Gray_579", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2047", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3731", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6233", "Pathoma_Husain_229"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 1138.9548778533936, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 3061.8348121643066, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1286, "output": 128}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00097", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "B", "explanation": "The clinical presentation and culture results indicate Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection. The lack of lasting immunity after infection is characteristic of this organism due to its ability to undergo antigenic variation of surface proteins, allowing it to evade the host immune response and cause reinfections. This is a well-established mechanism, though the provided evidence does not explicitly discuss it; however, among the options, antigenic variation is the best explanation for the lack of lasting immunity in gonorrhea.", "confidence": 0.9, "gold_answer": "B", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 13503.427982330322, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1192, "output": 866}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_5662", "Pharmacology_Katzung_5601", "First_Aid_Step1_148", "InternalMed_Harrison_5204", "InternalMed_Harrison_4675"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 833.3141803741455, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 12669.909000396729, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1192, "output": 866}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00098", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "B", "explanation": "The patient has significant neck pain after a motor vehicle accident, raising concern for potential cervical spine injury. Evidence [4] emphasizes that before moving an accident victim, a rapid examination for airway, breathing, circulation, and hemorrhage should be performed, and immobilization is standard to prevent further injury until imaging can be done. Cervical immobilization is the most appropriate immediate step.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "B", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 3836.7223739624023, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1083, "output": 99}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_3358", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4939", "Anatomy_Gray_554", "Neurology_Adams_7128", "First_Aid_Step2_1082"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 647.3631858825684, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 3189.164400100708, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1083, "output": 99}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
