{"question_id": "test_00103", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "A", "explanation": "The patient presents with altered mental status, hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, dry coarse skin, non-pitting edema, hyponatremia, and markedly elevated TSH with low T4 and T3, which are classic for myxedema coma. The other options are inconsistent: pheochromocytoma typically causes hypertension and tachycardia; septic shock usually presents with fever or hypothermia but not these specific skin and thyroid labs; tertiary hyperparathyroidism would show hypercalcemia, not hyponatremia and low thyroid hormones.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "A", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 9715.285778045654, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1333, "output": 360}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_20987", "Pharmacology_Katzung_6233", "InternalMed_Harrison_21134", "InternalMed_Harrison_24358", "InternalMed_Harrison_30506"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 603.7201881408691, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 9111.363172531128, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1333, "output": 360}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00104", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "B", "explanation": "The early diastolic blowing decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border, wide pulse pressure, and bounding pulses are classic for aortic regurgitation. The late diastolic rumbling murmur at the apex is likely an Austin Flint murmur, a functional murmur of mitral stenosis due to aortic regurgitation, as described in evidence [2]. The midsystolic murmur at the right upper sternal border may be an ejection flow murmur from increased stroke volume in chronic aortic regurgitation.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "B", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 9761.281728744507, "total_token_usage": {"input": 968, "output": 711}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_202", "First_Aid_Step1_296", "InternalMed_Harrison_2903", "InternalMed_Harrison_20987", "InternalMed_Harrison_19887"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 653.0909538269043, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 9107.992172241211, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 968, "output": 711}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
