{"question_id": "test_00176", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "D", "explanation": "Specificity is the proportion of patients without disease who have a negative test. Here, 600 patients without PD, 20 tested positive (false positives), so true negatives = 600 - 20 = 580. Thus specificity = 580 / (580 + 20). This matches choice D, as per definition in evidence 4.", "confidence": 1.0, "gold_answer": "D", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 5289.059162139893, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1002, "output": 459}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Neurology_Adams_8547", "Histology_Ross_1301", "InternalMed_Harrison_19671", "InternalMed_Harrison_238", "InternalMed_Harrison_6747"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 607.1133613586426, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 4681.716680526733, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1002, "output": 459}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00177", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "C", "explanation": "The patient is over 45, has chronic bilateral knee and wrist pain with morning stiffness lasting about 50 minutes, non-tender nodules on DIP joints (Heberden's nodes), thumb base tenderness, and knee crepitus—all classic for osteoarthritis. Evidence [3] states OA is the most common cause of chronic knee pain in persons over 45, and the physical findings match OA rather than inflammatory arthritis or other conditions.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "C", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 6714.119434356689, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1232, "output": 113}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Neurology_Adams_1720", "Neurology_Adams_11603", "InternalMed_Harrison_26084", "Pathology_Robbins_4851", "First_Aid_Step2_1245"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 687.8776550292969, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 6025.992870330811, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1232, "output": 113}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00178", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "D", "explanation": "A left C5 hemicord lesion (Brown-Séquard syndrome) typically causes ipsilateral loss of motor function and proprioception, and contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation (analgesia) due to crossing of the spinothalamic tract. This aligns with the expected finding of right-sided analgesia.", "confidence": 0.9, "gold_answer": "D", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 15936.74612045288, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1248, "output": 1743}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Anatomy_Gray_554", "Pharmacology_Katzung_4939", "InternalMed_Harrison_20987", "Pharmacology_Katzung_2624", "Anatomy_Gray_270"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 849.0102291107178, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 15087.547063827515, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1248, "output": 1743}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00179", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "C", "explanation": "The patient's presentation of epididymitis (scrotal pain, tender testicle, relief with lifting, increased blood flow on ultrasound) with dysuria, frequency, and WBCs on urinalysis but no organisms on Gram stain is consistent with nongonococcal urethritis, most commonly caused by Chlamydia trachomatis. Evidence [5] describes gonococcal urethritis as having Gram-negative intracellular diplococci, which are absent here. Evidence [2] supports epididymo-orchitis as a complication of urethritis, and the absence of organisms on Gram stain points to chlamydial infection.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "C", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 6936.77544593811, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1353, "output": 533}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["First_Aid_Step2_1148", "Surgery_Schwartz_11647", "InternalMed_Harrison_3311", "InternalMed_Harrison_12211", "InternalMed_Harrison_12208"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 623.2781410217285, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 6313.29607963562, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1353, "output": 533}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00180", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "B", "explanation": "The patient presents with a bleeding tendency (epistaxis, bruising, petechiae), prolonged bleeding time, normal platelet count, normal coagulation times, and normal ristocetin aggregation. These findings are characteristic of Glanzmann thrombasthenia, a platelet function disorder. The normal ristocetin aggregation rules out Bernard-Soulier disease and von Willebrand disease, while the clinical presentation and absence of other features make scurvy less likely. The red cell changes (hypochromia, poikilocytosis) may indicate concurrent iron deficiency, but the primary bleeding disorder is Glanzmann thrombasthenia.", "confidence": 0.85, "gold_answer": "B", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 43324.55563545227, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1311, "output": 3677}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_1429", "Pediatrics_Nelson_722", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1378", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1394", "Pediatrics_Nelson_1436"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 1217.0679569244385, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 42107.28335380554, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1311, "output": 3677}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
