{"question_id": "test_00184", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "D", "explanation": "The patient presents with rapidly progressive dementia over 5 weeks, with memory impairment, attention deficits, and loss of daily function, consistent with Alzheimer's disease. Evidence supports use of anticholinesterase agents or memantine for Alzheimer's (source 1). Among the choices, memantine is specifically indicated for moderate to severe Alzheimer's dementia.", "confidence": 0.9, "gold_answer": "A", "is_correct": false, "total_latency_ms": 3748.0554580688477, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1362, "output": 94}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_6689", "Neurology_Adams_5968", "First_Aid_Step2_1018", "First_Aid_Step2_992", "InternalMed_Harrison_2047"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 867.4139976501465, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 2880.446195602417, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1362, "output": 94}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00185", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "D", "explanation": "The child has intermediate-risk neuroblastoma, a life-threatening condition for which neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by surgery is the established standard treatment. Parental refusal of potentially curative therapy places the child at significant risk of harm. In such cases, the physician's duty to protect the child's welfare may necessitate seeking a court order to override parental refusal and provide necessary treatment.", "confidence": 0.9, "gold_answer": "D", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 19295.243978500366, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1281, "output": 616}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_3323", "InternalMed_Harrison_7371", "First_Aid_Step2_973", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3377", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3357"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 677.1700382232666, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 18617.877960205078, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1281, "output": 616}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00186", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "B", "explanation": "Evidence [1] states that only the anticodon of a tRNA determines incorporation specificity, so a mischarged tRNA with a lysine anticodon (Lys-tRNA) charged with valine will incorporate valine at lysine codons, as the ribosome does not check the amino acid. The mRNA contains only lysine codons, so the mischarged tRNA will be incorporated at those codons.", "confidence": 1.0, "gold_answer": "B", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 15643.732070922852, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1169, "output": 987}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Biochemistry_Lippincott_1615", "Cell_Biology_Alberts_1632", "Cell_Biology_Alberts_29", "Cell_Biology_Alberts_1625", "Biochemistry_Lippincott_1607"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 1396.13938331604, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 14247.311353683472, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1169, "output": 987}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00187", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "B", "explanation": "The evidence states that NMDA-receptor antagonists like ketamine have shown promise in reducing opioid requirements in opioid-tolerant patients, which matches the scenario of a patient with dose-limiting side effects from morphine.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "B", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 7203.874588012695, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1035, "output": 64}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_702", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3462", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3448", "InternalMed_Harrison_30141", "Pharmacology_Katzung_3440"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 655.2493572235107, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 6548.445463180542, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1035, "output": 64}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00188", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "D", "explanation": "The patient's shoulder pain worsened by sleeping on the affected side, tenderness over the greater tuberosity and bicipital groove, limited range of motion, and X-ray findings of acromiohumeral narrowing with sclerosis and spurring are classic for rotator cuff impingement syndrome, a type of rotator cuff injury. This is supported by evidence [3] describing impingement in overhead athletes like tennis players, with pain at night and tenderness over the lateral humeral head. The positive rheumatoid factor is nonspecific and not typical for rheumatoid arthritis, which would show different X-ray findings (e.g., erosions) and is less likely given the patient's age and activity. Trauma is denied, and osteoarthritis rarely involves the glenohumeral joint without a traumatic cause.", "confidence": 0.9, "gold_answer": "D", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 15564.63098526001, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1254, "output": 806}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Neurology_Adams_1684", "InternalMed_Harrison_26008", "InternalMed_Harrison_26266", "InternalMed_Harrison_1572", "InternalMed_Harrison_26005"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 647.9525566101074, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 14916.463375091553, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1254, "output": 806}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00189", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "C", "explanation": "The patient's symptoms (binge eating, enlarged cheeks, calluses on dorsum of hands) suggest bulimia nervosa with self-induced vomiting. Erosion of tooth enamel is a classic finding due to repeated exposure to gastric acid. The evidence discusses amenorrhea in the context of eating disorders and stress, but does not directly list this finding; however, the clinical presentation points to bulimia, where dental erosion is expected.", "confidence": 0.9, "gold_answer": "C", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 4703.761339187622, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1120, "output": 110}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pediatrics_Nelson_1550", "Gynecology_Novak_5168", "Gynecology_Novak_2044", "Gynecology_Novak_5163", "Gynecology_Novak_5188"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 1063.8906955718994, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 3639.6403312683105, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1120, "output": 110}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00190", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "A", "explanation": "The patient's episodic chest discomfort, shortness of breath, shakiness, sweating, and anxiety about attacks in public are classic for panic disorder with agoraphobia. Evidence [4] lists anxiety state as a treatable chronic cause of chest discomfort, and first-line long-term pharmacotherapy for panic disorder is an SSRI such as citalopram.", "confidence": 0.8, "gold_answer": "A", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 28253.2958984375, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1095, "output": 1871}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Pharmacology_Katzung_914", "InternalMed_Harrison_1323", "Pediatrics_Nelson_3218", "InternalMed_Harrison_1344", "InternalMed_Harrison_1310"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 1209.1279029846191, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 27043.885469436646, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1095, "output": 1871}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00191", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "C", "explanation": "The stillborn infant's findings (absence of sacrum and lumbar vertebrae, small pelvis, club feet) are characteristic of caudal regression syndrome, which is strongly associated with maternal diabetes. Evidence [4] mentions diabetes as a modifiable risk factor for stillbirth, though not directly linking to this anomaly. Among the options, maternal diabetes is the most established predisposing factor for this condition.", "confidence": 0.9, "gold_answer": "C", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 13745.23115158081, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1048, "output": 1033}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["Obstentrics_Williams_4366", "Obstentrics_Williams_4418", "Obstentrics_Williams_4359", "Obstentrics_Williams_4416", "Obstentrics_Williams_4995"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 683.3052635192871, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 13061.681985855103, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1048, "output": 1033}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
{"question_id": "test_00192", "variant": "v1", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash", "predicted_answer": "D", "explanation": "The evidence shows that CD4+ T cell count modifies the risk of opportunistic infections and the effectiveness of interventions (e.g., prophylaxis discontinuation depends on CD4+ count). Here, the effect of cART adherence on DLBCL risk differs by CD4+ stratum, which is a classic example of effect modification.", "confidence": 0.95, "gold_answer": "D", "is_correct": true, "total_latency_ms": 8764.295816421509, "total_token_usage": {"input": 1056, "output": 89}, "estimated_cost": null, "evidence_used": ["InternalMed_Harrison_14943", "InternalMed_Harrison_16715", "InternalMed_Harrison_15240", "Pathology_Robbins_3260", "InternalMed_Harrison_15107"], "retrieval_latency_ms": 688.7421607971191, "retrieval_token_usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0}, "iteration_count": null, "verifier_verdict": null, "verdict_history": null, "stopped_after_max_iterations": null, "reasoning_latency_ms": 8075.291395187378, "reasoning_token_usage": {"input": 1056, "output": 89}, "verifier_latency_ms": null, "verifier_token_usage": null, "query_rewrite_count": null, "rewriter_latency_ms": null, "rewriter_token_usage": null, "stm_scope_used": null, "loop_history_length": null, "ltm_facts_retrieved": null, "ltm_write_triggered": null, "user_id": null}
